# Intent: the shared game-over screen gets a graphical overhaul (docs/design/game-over-look.md), as a look LEVEL: look 0 is today's screen unchanged, 1 the new look still (the default candidate, owner 2026-11-19), 2 the new look with a light motion (the emblem stands still; confetti in two narrow columns, rain, a clasp squeeze, a clock hand and the count-up, each redrawn as a rectangle of its own, at most 3 a step) that stays a bench level until it meets its gates on the unit. The new look is drawn by ONE draw callback on one canvas object (2 objects, was about 12): a gradient backdrop per outcome, a big emblem from primitives (a trophy with turning rays and confetti for a win, a droopy kind face under a rain cloud for a loss, two arms meeting in a clasp for a draw, a finish flag or a clock for a neutral end, a crown over a ribbon with sparkles and gold confetti for a new best), the result word with a shadow, the score counting up, the best chip, two 56 px buttons with a gradient, a rim and a pressed state. Six languages (en, es, fr, de, pt, it) for the words, a flourish of tones per outcome in maipai_game_over_core.h. The callers are unchanged: build() and set_pop() keep their arguments; MGO_POP_MS grows to 1800 so the games keep calling set_pop while the motion plays. self.game.bench_over (bench builds only) shows any outcome at any look and answers the draw and flush times.
# Placement: unit
# Upstream: firmware/main/hal/maipai_game_over.h, maipai_game_over_core.h and maipai_grid.cc (ours, patches UX15, UX15c, UX26)
# Check after a flash: the game-over screen of every game shows the new still look (look 1, the default candidate: gradient backdrop, a drawn emblem, the result word, score and best chip, two gradient buttons); look 0 (today's) and look 2 (the lighter motion: confetti in two columns for a win or a new best, rain, a squeeze, a clock hand, a count-up) are reached with self.game.bench_over. On a bench image: self.game.bench_over with look 0, 1 and 2 for result 1 to 5 and new_best 0 and 1 answers us_mean, us_worst, objects and the internal free before and during (docs/design/game-over-look.md, "Bench"); the screen shows and goes away.
# Kconfig: none
# Memory cost: look 0 none; look 1 and 2 two LVGL objects and one state of about 150 bytes while the screen shows (the old screen is about 12 objects, an estimated 1.7 KB internal); the draw code is about 6 KB of flash in the bench build only when the look is compiled in (always, as a dead branch at look 0).

